1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,420
On the evening of December 25th,

2
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:06,720
1991, the red flag over the Kremlin came

3
00:00:06,740 --> 00:00:08,180
down for the last time.

4
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:11,460
Within hours, the largest country on Earth

5
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:14,740
was gone, and in its place stood fifteen

6
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:15,660
new nations.

7
00:00:16,140 --> 00:00:17,800
But here is the strange part.

8
00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:20,940
The Soviet Union was not built from fifteen

9
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:21,520
peoples.

10
00:00:21,940 --> 00:00:23,880
It was a patchwork of more than a hundred

11
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:26,500
nationalities, organized into fifty-three

12
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:27,780
official homelands.

13
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:30,600
Tatarstan had more than twice as many people

14
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:33,780
as Estonia, yet Estonia became a country

15
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:35,380
and Tatarstan did not.

16
00:00:35,860 --> 00:00:38,240
Chechnya would fight two brutal wars over

17
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:40,940
independence, and end up back under Moscow's

18
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:41,360
control.

19
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:44,380
So why fifteen, and not twenty,

20
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:45,420
or fifty?

21
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:47,560
The answer had been written into the Soviet

22
00:00:47,580 --> 00:00:50,500
system decades earlier, by Joseph Stalin

23
00:00:50,520 --> 00:00:51,000
himself.

24
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:53,600
This is why the Soviet Union broke into

25
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:55,320
exactly fifteen countries.

26
00:00:59,665 --> 00:01:02,165
When the Bolsheviks won the Russian Civil

27
00:01:02,185 --> 00:01:04,225
War, they inherited a problem.

28
00:01:04,725 --> 00:01:07,145
The old Russian Empire had been a prison

29
00:01:07,165 --> 00:01:10,065
of nations, and many of those nations had

30
00:01:10,085 --> 00:01:11,305
briefly broken free.

31
00:01:11,985 --> 00:01:14,365
Lenin's solution was a clever compromise.

32
00:01:14,905 --> 00:01:18,025
Ironically, Stalin, then in charge of nationality

33
00:01:18,045 --> 00:01:21,105
affairs, wanted every republic to simply

34
00:01:21,125 --> 00:01:23,785
join Russia as autonomous republics.

35
00:01:23,805 --> 00:01:27,265
Lenin overruled him and insisted on a union

36
00:01:27,285 --> 00:01:29,405
of supposedly equal partners.

37
00:01:29,905 --> 00:01:35,015
On December 30th, 1922, four Soviet republics,

38
00:01:35,035 --> 00:01:38,725
Russia, Ukraine, Belarus and Transcaucasia,

39
00:01:38,745 --> 00:01:42,425
signed a treaty creating the Union of Soviet

40
00:01:42,445 --> 00:01:43,865
Socialist Republics.

41
00:01:44,065 --> 00:01:46,945
On paper, it was a voluntary union of equal

42
00:01:46,965 --> 00:01:47,425
nations.

43
00:01:47,725 --> 00:01:50,385
In reality, it was a matryoshka,

44
00:01:50,665 --> 00:01:51,885
a Russian nesting doll.

45
00:01:51,905 --> 00:01:54,845
At the top stood the union republics.

46
00:01:55,045 --> 00:01:58,065
Inside them sat autonomous republics.

47
00:01:58,425 --> 00:02:00,525
Inside those, autonomous regions,

48
00:02:00,545 --> 00:02:02,645
and smaller autonomous districts.

49
00:02:03,165 --> 00:02:06,605
By 1989, there were fifteen union republics,

50
00:02:06,625 --> 00:02:08,585
twenty autonomous republics,

51
00:02:08,605 --> 00:02:11,665
eight autonomous regions and ten autonomous

52
00:02:11,685 --> 00:02:12,205
districts.

53
00:02:12,225 --> 00:02:14,645
Every layer had its own borders,

54
00:02:15,125 --> 00:02:18,505
its own capital, and its own official nationality.

55
00:02:18,525 --> 00:02:20,515
But only the outer shell,

56
00:02:20,535 --> 00:02:24,065
the union republics, held one extraordinary

57
00:02:24,085 --> 00:02:24,605
privilege.

58
00:02:24,945 --> 00:02:27,645
The Soviet constitution guaranteed each

59
00:02:27,665 --> 00:02:30,305
of them the right to freely leave the Union.

60
00:02:30,685 --> 00:02:33,705
For decades, that right meant nothing.

61
00:02:34,205 --> 00:02:36,625
Any real attempt to use it would have ended

62
00:02:36,645 --> 00:02:37,865
in a prison camp.

63
00:02:37,885 --> 00:02:39,385
But the words were there,

64
00:02:39,625 --> 00:02:41,165
written in black and white,

65
00:02:41,505 --> 00:02:41,785
waiting.

66
00:02:44,074 --> 00:02:47,394
By the mid-1930s, the map had already grown.

67
00:02:47,854 --> 00:02:50,534
Uzbekistan and Turkmenistan became union

68
00:02:50,554 --> 00:02:54,714
republics in 1924, Tajikistan in 1929.

69
00:02:55,414 --> 00:02:59,714
Then, in 1936, Stalin unveiled a new constitution.

70
00:03:00,174 --> 00:03:02,734
Kazakhstan and Kyrgyzstan were promoted

71
00:03:02,754 --> 00:03:05,794
to full union republics, and the Transcaucasian

72
00:03:05,814 --> 00:03:07,754
republic was split into Georgia,

73
00:03:08,114 --> 00:03:09,634
Armenia and Azerbaijan.

74
00:03:10,214 --> 00:03:11,194
That made eleven.

75
00:03:11,214 --> 00:03:13,714
Other nations asked for the same promotion,

76
00:03:14,154 --> 00:03:16,494
so Stalin explained the rules in a famous

77
00:03:16,514 --> 00:03:16,974
speech.

78
00:03:17,294 --> 00:03:18,934
To become a union republic,

79
00:03:19,454 --> 00:03:21,094
a nation needed three things.

80
00:03:21,534 --> 00:03:24,154
First, it had to lie on the outer border

81
00:03:24,174 --> 00:03:25,614
of the Soviet Union.

82
00:03:26,154 --> 00:03:27,594
After all, Stalin argued,

83
00:03:27,994 --> 00:03:30,554
a republic surrounded on every side by Soviet

84
00:03:30,574 --> 00:03:32,734
territory could never actually leave.

85
00:03:33,534 --> 00:03:36,234
Second, its namesake nationality had to

86
00:03:36,254 --> 00:03:37,714
form a compact majority.

87
00:03:38,274 --> 00:03:40,694
And third, its population had to be large

88
00:03:40,714 --> 00:03:43,394
enough, at least around one million people.

89
00:03:44,034 --> 00:03:45,574
Now look at Tatarstan.

90
00:03:45,974 --> 00:03:49,454
By 1989, it had about three point six million

91
00:03:49,474 --> 00:03:52,541
people, while Estonia had about one point

92
00:03:52,561 --> 00:03:53,214
six million.

93
00:03:53,554 --> 00:03:56,054
A proud history, its own language,

94
00:03:56,074 --> 00:03:58,374
but it sits deep inside Russia,

95
00:03:58,574 --> 00:04:00,654
hundreds of kilometers from any foreign

96
00:04:00,674 --> 00:04:01,134
frontier.

97
00:04:01,554 --> 00:04:04,534
By Stalin's first rule, it could never qualify.

98
00:04:05,174 --> 00:04:07,454
The same was true for Bashkortostan,

99
00:04:07,474 --> 00:04:09,794
Chuvashia and the other republics along

100
00:04:09,814 --> 00:04:10,394
the Volga.

101
00:04:10,714 --> 00:04:13,234
In practice, Moscow applied these rules

102
00:04:13,254 --> 00:04:14,734
when it suited Moscow.

103
00:04:15,294 --> 00:04:17,974
Kazakhstan was promoted even though Kazakhs

104
00:04:17,994 --> 00:04:20,154
no longer made up a majority of their own

105
00:04:20,174 --> 00:04:20,674
republic.

106
00:04:21,074 --> 00:04:22,614
But the principle was set.

107
00:04:24,748 --> 00:04:28,328
Geography, not identity, decided which nations

108
00:04:28,348 --> 00:04:29,408
got the top rank.

109
00:04:29,948 --> 00:04:31,388
The map kept moving.

110
00:04:31,948 --> 00:04:35,488
In 1940, the Soviet Union annexed Estonia,

111
00:04:35,968 --> 00:04:38,968
Latvia and Lithuania, and created the Moldavian

112
00:04:38,988 --> 00:04:41,868
republic from territory taken from Romania.

113
00:04:42,288 --> 00:04:44,648
It also created a Karelo-Finnish republic

114
00:04:44,668 --> 00:04:46,048
on the border with Finland,

115
00:04:46,408 --> 00:04:48,808
a republic designed to absorb Finland if

116
00:04:48,828 --> 00:04:49,568
it ever fell.

117
00:04:50,048 --> 00:04:51,968
That brought the total to sixteen.

118
00:04:51,988 --> 00:04:55,388
But Finland never fell, and Finns made up

119
00:04:55,408 --> 00:04:57,948
only a small minority of the new republic.

120
00:04:58,548 --> 00:05:02,448
So in 1956, Moscow quietly downgraded it

121
00:05:02,468 --> 00:05:04,648
to an autonomous republic inside Russia.

122
00:05:04,668 --> 00:05:07,788
Sixteen became fifteen, and the number never

123
00:05:07,808 --> 00:05:08,628
changed again.

124
00:05:09,008 --> 00:05:11,188
Two years earlier, in 1954,

125
00:05:11,728 --> 00:05:13,608
another quiet decision had been made.

126
00:05:13,988 --> 00:05:16,528
The Crimean peninsula was transferred from

127
00:05:16,548 --> 00:05:19,088
the Russian republic to the Ukrainian republic.

128
00:05:19,108 --> 00:05:22,348
At the time, it seemed like an administrative

129
00:05:22,368 --> 00:05:22,968
detail.

130
00:05:23,448 --> 00:05:25,728
Nobody imagined that these internal lines

131
00:05:25,748 --> 00:05:28,048
would one day become international borders.

132
00:05:30,313 --> 00:05:34,213
In the late 1980s, Mikhail Gorbachev loosened

133
00:05:34,233 --> 00:05:35,253
the grip of fear.

134
00:05:36,113 --> 00:05:38,853
Suddenly, the old promises written in the

135
00:05:38,873 --> 00:05:40,853
constitution started to matter.

136
00:05:41,433 --> 00:05:44,933
In August 1989, some two million people

137
00:05:44,953 --> 00:05:47,593
joined hands in a human chain stretching

138
00:05:47,613 --> 00:05:50,553
across Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania.

139
00:05:51,073 --> 00:05:54,773
On March 11th, 1990, Lithuania declared

140
00:05:54,793 --> 00:05:57,173
that it was restoring its independence.

141
00:05:57,193 --> 00:06:00,113
Moscow responded with a law that made leaving

142
00:06:00,133 --> 00:06:02,913
extremely difficult, demanding referendums,

143
00:06:02,933 --> 00:06:05,673
two-thirds majorities and a transition of

144
00:06:05,693 --> 00:06:06,793
up to five years.

145
00:06:07,173 --> 00:06:10,433
In March 1991, the Kremlin held a referendum

146
00:06:10,453 --> 00:06:11,793
on preserving the Union.

147
00:06:12,513 --> 00:06:14,593
Six republics refused to take part:

148
00:06:14,953 --> 00:06:17,133
the three Baltic states, Georgia,

149
00:06:17,493 --> 00:06:19,053
Armenia and Moldova.

150
00:06:19,513 --> 00:06:23,373
Then, in August 1991, hardliners tried to

151
00:06:23,393 --> 00:06:24,753
seize power in Moscow.

152
00:06:25,073 --> 00:06:27,153
The coup collapsed in three days,

153
00:06:27,173 --> 00:06:29,713
and so did the authority of the center.

154
00:06:30,293 --> 00:06:33,793
Within weeks, republic after republic declared

155
00:06:33,813 --> 00:06:34,593
independence.

156
00:06:35,133 --> 00:06:38,053
In September, Moscow recognized the independence

157
00:06:38,073 --> 00:06:39,413
of the Baltic states.

158
00:06:39,873 --> 00:06:42,353
On December 1st, more than ninety percent

159
00:06:42,373 --> 00:06:44,753
of Ukrainian voters chose independence.

160
00:06:45,433 --> 00:06:47,353
A week later, the leaders of Russia,

161
00:06:47,593 --> 00:06:50,073
Ukraine and Belarus met in a hunting lodge

162
00:06:50,093 --> 00:06:52,653
and declared that the Soviet Union no longer

163
00:06:52,673 --> 00:06:53,273
existed.

164
00:06:53,653 --> 00:06:56,413
On December 21st, eleven republics confirmed

165
00:06:56,433 --> 00:06:56,733
it.

166
00:06:56,733 --> 00:06:59,373
Four days later, Gorbachev resigned.

167
00:06:59,813 --> 00:07:01,373
The fifteen union republics,

168
00:07:01,533 --> 00:07:03,193
the outer shells of the matryoshka,

169
00:07:03,213 --> 00:07:05,853
stepped onto the world stage as fifteen

170
00:07:05,873 --> 00:07:06,933
independent states.

171
00:07:09,131 --> 00:07:10,711
But what about the dolls inside?

172
00:07:11,171 --> 00:07:12,191
They tried too.

173
00:07:12,671 --> 00:07:15,231
Tatarstan declared its sovereignty in 1990,

174
00:07:15,571 --> 00:07:18,991
and in 1992 about sixty-one percent of voters

175
00:07:19,011 --> 00:07:20,651
backed a sovereign status.

176
00:07:21,031 --> 00:07:23,351
But with no foreign border and Russia on

177
00:07:23,371 --> 00:07:26,231
every side, it settled for a special treaty

178
00:07:26,251 --> 00:07:28,511
with Moscow in 1994.

179
00:07:29,091 --> 00:07:30,131
Chechnya went further.

180
00:07:30,631 --> 00:07:34,791
In November 1991, it declared full independence.

181
00:07:35,251 --> 00:07:37,771
The result was two devastating wars,

182
00:07:38,071 --> 00:07:43,271
from 1994 to 1996, and again from 1999,

183
00:07:44,431 --> 00:07:47,591
which ended with Chechnya firmly under Moscow's

184
00:07:47,611 --> 00:07:48,091
control.

185
00:07:48,671 --> 00:07:52,231
Abkhazia, South Ossetia, Nagorno-Karabakh

186
00:07:52,251 --> 00:07:54,731
and Transnistria also broke away from the

187
00:07:54,751 --> 00:07:56,911
new states around them, leading to bloody

188
00:07:56,931 --> 00:07:59,491
conflicts and decades of frozen disputes.

189
00:08:00,071 --> 00:08:02,491
None of them won broad international recognition.

190
00:08:02,831 --> 00:08:03,171
Why?

191
00:08:03,511 --> 00:08:05,511
When the new states formed their Commonwealth

192
00:08:05,531 --> 00:08:08,711
in December 1991, they pledged to respect

193
00:08:08,731 --> 00:08:11,571
each other's territorial integrity and existing

194
00:08:11,591 --> 00:08:12,071
borders.

195
00:08:12,511 --> 00:08:14,931
And the wider world followed an old legal

196
00:08:14,951 --> 00:08:17,551
principle called uti possidetis juris,

197
00:08:17,911 --> 00:08:20,291
which means that new states inherit the

198
00:08:20,311 --> 00:08:22,791
borders they held as administrative units.

199
00:08:23,391 --> 00:08:26,211
The fifteen union republics had legal borders

200
00:08:26,231 --> 00:08:28,271
and a constitutional right to leave.

201
00:08:28,531 --> 00:08:30,471
The units inside them did not.

202
00:08:31,031 --> 00:08:34,171
So the lines drawn by Soviet planners became

203
00:08:34,191 --> 00:08:36,691
the borders of the post-Soviet world.

204
00:08:38,833 --> 00:08:41,633
In the end, the Soviet Union did not break

205
00:08:41,653 --> 00:08:43,513
apart along the lines of language,

206
00:08:43,993 --> 00:08:45,093
religion or history.

207
00:08:45,213 --> 00:08:47,833
It broke along the seams that its own founders

208
00:08:47,853 --> 00:08:49,253
had stitched into it.

209
00:08:49,713 --> 00:08:51,893
Stalin built a system where the right to

210
00:08:51,913 --> 00:08:54,573
independence existed only on paper,

211
00:08:54,593 --> 00:08:57,353
and only for nations on the edge of the

212
00:08:57,373 --> 00:08:57,613
map.

213
00:08:58,173 --> 00:09:01,093
He never imagined that paper would one day

214
00:09:01,113 --> 00:09:01,813
be enough.

215
00:09:02,173 --> 00:09:04,413
When the moment came, only the outermost

216
00:09:04,433 --> 00:09:05,653
dolls could walk away.

217
00:09:05,673 --> 00:09:08,973
And that is why one superpower became exactly

218
00:09:08,993 --> 00:09:11,833
fifteen countries, no more and no less.
